WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.335 --> 00:00:02.235
How to translate comparative passages.

2
00:00:02.265 --> 00:00:04.555
Okay, so you basically do it the same way

3
00:00:04.555 --> 00:00:05.715
that you translate any passage.

4
00:00:05.775 --> 00:00:07.155
You know, your full RTQ process

5
00:00:07.495 --> 00:00:09.115
and following the instructions on whatever

6
00:00:09.115 --> 00:00:10.195
translation drill you're doing.

7
00:00:10.195 --> 00:00:12.115
It would apply to RC paragraphs the same

8
00:00:12.115 --> 00:00:13.195
way it would anything else.

9
00:00:13.695 --> 00:00:15.275
The only thing is you wanna make sure

10
00:00:15.275 --> 00:00:18.875
that you button up passage A, when you finish passage A.

11
00:00:18.895 --> 00:00:21.795
So for instance, if I'm doing decaf, let's say, um,

12
00:00:21.935 --> 00:00:23.915
and I have, you know, my paragraph repetitions

13
00:00:23.915 --> 00:00:27.075
or things like that, I will button up and finish passage A

14
00:00:27.375 --> 00:00:28.795
before I do passage B.

15
00:00:28.795 --> 00:00:31.355
And I'll even infer out a main point on passage A

16
00:00:31.415 --> 00:00:34.795
before I go into passage B, so that I have all that nice

17
00:00:34.795 --> 00:00:38.875
and tied up with a bow before I go into passage B Territory.

18
00:00:39.145 --> 00:00:41.715
Just because you wanna make sure that you weren't blending,

19
00:00:42.015 --> 00:00:44.675
uh, the ideas between passage A and B.

20
00:00:44.675 --> 00:00:46.275
That's why it's really good to be like, alright,

21
00:00:46.295 --> 00:00:47.395
and passage A is done.

22
00:00:47.515 --> 00:00:50.355
I put a bow on it, I'm over with it. Okay, cool.

23
00:00:50.615 --> 00:00:52.235
Now I have it in the back of my mind

24
00:00:52.235 --> 00:00:54.995
and it's gonna inform my perspective on passage B as I go,

25
00:00:55.055 --> 00:00:57.915
but it's gonna be separate, separate, not blended together.

26
00:00:58.095 --> 00:00:59.315
And so that's really, really important.

27
00:00:59.785 --> 00:01:02.195
Next thing is utilizing that passage, a perspective

28
00:01:02.195 --> 00:01:03.635
that you have in passage B.

29
00:01:03.635 --> 00:01:07.355
There's a really cool little tip that I, I stole from one

30
00:01:07.355 --> 00:01:09.675
of my students in, I think it was 2019.

31
00:01:09.735 --> 00:01:11.555
He told me he was doing this. I was like, bro,

32
00:01:11.765 --> 00:01:13.675
we're gonna have everybody do this 'cause this is great.

33
00:01:14.215 --> 00:01:17.275
Um, it is highlighting topics

34
00:01:17.575 --> 00:01:21.755
or words that are mentioned in passage A while you're

35
00:01:21.755 --> 00:01:22.915
reading passage B.

36
00:01:23.135 --> 00:01:26.635
And so let's say the passage A mentioned muskrats,

37
00:01:26.735 --> 00:01:28.875
and then I'm in passage B and I see muskrats.

38
00:01:29.035 --> 00:01:32.435
I give that muskrats a little highlight. I love that idea.

39
00:01:32.675 --> 00:01:35.115
'cause it just automatically gives you the answer to all

40
00:01:35.115 --> 00:01:36.275
of those questions about what was

41
00:01:36.275 --> 00:01:37.475
mentioned in both passages.

42
00:01:37.475 --> 00:01:39.555
Like you immediately would have a reference for it.

43
00:01:39.855 --> 00:01:42.235
And then you can assume in those questions as well

44
00:01:42.265 --> 00:01:44.475
that if something isn't highlighted,

45
00:01:46.275 --> 00:01:47.375
it might not be the answer.

46
00:01:47.375 --> 00:01:48.615
Right? You know, it would be,

47
00:01:48.755 --> 00:01:50.895
it would be at least less likely to be the answer.

48
00:01:51.195 --> 00:01:54.375
Of course here you do wanna be careful about making sure you

49
00:01:54.375 --> 00:01:56.135
know, you don't have exact word itis

50
00:01:56.235 --> 00:01:58.935
and you're only doing like, you know, letters matching.

51
00:01:59.425 --> 00:02:01.335
Don't do that. Um, make sure that

52
00:02:01.435 --> 00:02:03.415
as you're highlighting your thinking conceptually,

53
00:02:03.415 --> 00:02:05.055
if they say muskrat versus, I don't know,

54
00:02:05.055 --> 00:02:06.655
like small mammal or something.

55
00:02:07.185 --> 00:02:09.375
Small mammals would be mentioned if muskrats were mentioned.

56
00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:12.095
So that's an example of, you'd still wanna highlight

57
00:02:12.095 --> 00:02:14.095
that even though it's not just the word muskrats,

58
00:02:14.095 --> 00:02:16.215
it is a small mammal, which a muskrat is as well.

59
00:02:16.475 --> 00:02:18.615
And so that will help you with those sorts of questions.

60
00:02:18.635 --> 00:02:20.375
And then also the questions like

61
00:02:20.375 --> 00:02:23.455
what was mentioned only in passage A and not in passage B.

62
00:02:23.605 --> 00:02:26.615
Well, if I know muskrat was mentioned in both, okay,

63
00:02:26.845 --> 00:02:28.295
then I can cross this off

64
00:02:28.375 --> 00:02:29.615
'cause I know it's mentioned in B as well.

65
00:02:30.275 --> 00:02:31.855
All right, so that's one optimization.

66
00:02:31.875 --> 00:02:33.255
And then here's the last one.

67
00:02:33.475 --> 00:02:36.495
We wanna make sure you have at least one quip on each

68
00:02:36.495 --> 00:02:39.855
paragraph of passage B that directly mentions passage A

69
00:02:39.995 --> 00:02:44.175
or some kind of content in passage A that will allow you

70
00:02:44.175 --> 00:02:47.095
to start really interrelating the two passages together,

71
00:02:47.275 --> 00:02:49.015
seeing the relationships kind

72
00:02:49.015 --> 00:02:50.735
of like you would when you quip upwards in

73
00:02:50.795 --> 00:02:53.855
or within a stimulus, uh, as you go, which can be really,

74
00:02:53.855 --> 00:02:55.495
really helpful for those questions about, you know,

75
00:02:55.635 --> 00:02:57.375
how the passages vary from each other.

76
00:02:57.605 --> 00:02:58.875
What passage

77
00:02:59.205 --> 00:03:00.685
B author would say, back to passage,

78
00:03:00.885 --> 00:03:02.205
a author, or vice versa.

79
00:03:02.585 --> 00:03:04.845
If you're already putting the passages in conversation

80
00:03:04.845 --> 00:03:06.645
with each other during your translation,

81
00:03:06.745 --> 00:03:08.805
you'll have a massive leg up ahead

82
00:03:08.805 --> 00:03:10.725
of the game on those sorts of questions.

83
00:03:10.725 --> 00:03:11.885
And so I hope this helps.

84
00:03:11.885 --> 00:03:13.685
Just a few quick tips on comparatives.

